${macro.generated_by(self.path)}

=[ @telaion/theme ]

Design tokens, layer semantics, physical shadow models, and Tailwind preset for Tomet and Telaion editors.

==[ Install ]

```bash
pnpm add @telaion/theme
```

==[ Use ]

### Design tokens

Import `tokens.css` into your root stylesheet or application entry point:

```css
@import "@telaion/theme/tokens.css";
```

### Tailwind CSS (v4)

If using Tailwind CSS v4, also import `tailwind.css` to enable `@theme` mappings (such as `bg-tln-canvas`, `text-tln-fg`, `border-tln-border`, etc.):

```css
@import "tailwindcss";
@import "@telaion/theme/tokens.css";
@import "@telaion/theme/tailwind.css";
```

Every token is a utility, named after it with the `tln-` prefix (`--tln-fg-muted` is `text-tln-fg-muted`; the shadows are `shadow-tln-*`, the radius `rounded-tln`, the fonts `font-tln-sans` and `font-tln-mono`, the sizes `text-tln-xs` to `text-tln-2xl`, the weights `font-tln-medium` and `font-tln-strong`, the line of read text `leading-tln-prose`, the width `max-w-tln-measure`, the page `bg-tln-paper`). The utilities read the token itself, so a subtree with `data-theme` or an overridden token keeps working. Tailwind's own color palette, shadows, radii and font families are emptied, so a class such as `bg-stone-800`, `shadow-lg` or `rounded-md` does not exist: the tokens are the only ones. Layout, box sizes, tracking and arbitrary values are untouched. Tailwind's bare `transition` follows the design (`--default-transition-duration` and `--default-transition-timing-function` are the duration and the easing of the tokens). Two utilities are more than a value: `outline-tln-focus`, the focus outline of every control, and `transition-tln`, the transition of the design, off for a person who asks for less motion.

### TypeScript utilities

```ts
import { applyTheme, getEffectiveTheme } from "@telaion/theme";

// Switch between "system", "light", and "dark"
applyTheme(document.documentElement, "dark");
```

==[ Five Layers ]

-| `well` -- What is typed into (inputs, CodeMirror editors). Inward shadow.
-| `canvas` -- Ground surface. No shadow.
-| `content` -- What is read (cards, sidebars). Outward content shadow.
-| `action` -- What acts (buttons, tabs). One layer above what holds it.
-| `float` -- What floats above the page (modals, dialogs, popovers). Scrim and float shadow.

==[ Shape and height ]

Two tokens set how the whole design feels, and an application overrides them on `:root` (or on any element, for its subtree).

-| `--tln-radius` -- the one radius of the design. Small parts (a checkbox, a switch, the thumb of a slider) follow it up to half of their shortest side, so a high radius makes them round and never makes them bulge.
-| `--tln-height` -- how high a raised layer stands above the ground, a plain number: `1` is the design, `0` is flat. It scales how far every shadow falls and how soft the ambient one is. The lit edges do not scale. A shadow is only ever a multiple of the sun's offset (`--tln-sun-shadow-x` and `--tln-sun-shadow-y`, 1px when nothing sets them), so it always falls the way the sun says.

==[ Motion ]

-| `--tln-duration`, `--tln-ease` and `--tln-transition` (both together) are the motion of a change of state: a colour, an opacity. It does not overshoot.
-| `--tln-track` is how fast a surface follows the light of the pointer. It is quick, so that a shadow does not trail behind the pointer.
-| Every motion is off for a person who asks for less of it (`prefers-reduced-motion`).

Only the shadow tokens are declared on every element, because they depend on the sun and the lamp of each element. Every other token is declared once on `:root`.

